# No claim without a source

The owner's instruction, recorded 2026-10-02, after reading the work of nearly two slots: a scout said
a pocket was good, the UI/UX work said the design was good, and **none of them answered with facts**.
Each had answered from memory and presented the memory as a finding. That is the defect this rule
stops, and it is the factory's most expensive one precisely because it is invisible — a confident
sentence and a measured one look identical in a report, and only one of them is worth acting on.

The rule is one sentence. **Before a factual sentence leaves you — in chat, in a report, in a proposal,
in a review, in a code comment, in a decision — you must be able to name what you read or ran that
makes it true. If you cannot name it, you do not know it, and you say so instead.**

## The four labels, and what each one requires

The factory already carries this vocabulary (`docs/ideation/README.md`, `docs/slot-differentiators.md`,
`docs/research/measured-facts.json`). It is not decoration: the label is the difference between a claim
a reader can check and a claim a reader must trust on faith.

| Label | You may use it only when | What must accompany it |
|---|---|---|
| `MEASURED` | you ran a command, read a file, or observed the artifact **in this session** | the command and its observed output, or the `path:line` |
| `ON RECORD` | a named source states it and you read that source | the source, and the number or quote it gave |
| `INFERRED` | it follows from other labelled facts | the facts it follows from, and the step between them |
| `UNVERIFIED` | you did not check it | the exact command or file that would decide it, and who can run it |

`UNVERIFIED` is a **result**, not a failure. It is the honest answer to a question you could not
settle, and it is strictly better than a confident sentence that turns out to be invented. What is
forbidden is presenting an unverified claim under any other label.

## What is banned

- **An invented fact.** A path, a symbol, a line number, an API name, an artifact version, a price, a
  policy quote, an install count or a test result you did not read. If you believe it but did not
  check it, that is `UNVERIFIED` — never a stated fact.
- **Filling a gap with the plausible default.** "The theme is probably Material 3", "that file should
  hold the guard", "the build likely passed" — each is a guess wearing a fact's clothes. Go read it,
  or label it.
- **Paraphrasing a document as if citing it.** Name the file and the line. A summary of a source is an
  inference *about* the source, not the source.
- **A verdict on your own artifact.** "I built it well", "the tests are green", "this is ready" are
  self-reports. `AGENTS.md` already forbids a builder's `PASS` about its own work and
  `harness/roles/builder.md` repeats it; this rule extends that to **every** claim a worker makes
  about anything it produced.
- **A recommendation with no rejected alternative.** "This is the better choice" is not a decision
  input. State what you chose, what you rejected, and the fact that separated them — the same
  discipline `.cursor/rules/ideation-gate.mdc` requires of a monetization archetype and
  `.cursor/rules/app-naming.mdc` requires of a name.
- **"Probably", "should be", "seems", "I believe", "it works"** presented as fact rather than labelled
  as an inference or left unverified.

## How a claim is carried

A worker's report is read by an orchestrator that cannot see the worker's session, so the evidence
travels with the claim. Every report ends with a claim ledger:

```text
CLAIM                                        LABEL       EVIDENCE
the Scaffold discards its innerPadding       MEASURED    Surface.kt:24
predictive back is declared                  MEASURED    `rg enableOnBackInvokedCallback app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`
the shelf pays                               MEASURED    enrich-market.mjs, cashbook 4.5★ / 145000 reviews
the wedge will convert                       UNVERIFIED  no priced comparable found; needs one to decide
```

An orchestrator rejects a report whose rows carry no label, whose `MEASURED` row names no command and
no `path:line`, or whose conclusion rests on a row labelled `UNVERIFIED`. **A conclusion is only as
strong as its weakest load-bearing row.**

## Where this is dispatched from

A subagent never sees the orchestrator's conversation, so a rule it is not told to follow is a rule it
cannot follow. **Every dispatch brief states this duty**, and the brief is where the claim ledger is
required, because the worker cannot infer it (`.cursor/rules/parallel-execution.mdc`,
`harness/dual-agent.md`).

## The honest limit

This rule is **behavioural**, and a behavioural rule is the easiest to violate and the hardest to
detect. What a program can check is that the duty is *declared*: that this file loads
(`alwaysApply: true`), that every role card under `harness/roles/` binds it, and that every agent card
under `.cursor/agents/`, `.codex/agents/` and `.claude/agents/` points at it. That check is
`harness.groundedClaims`, and it is why those cards cannot quietly drop the rule. What no program can
check is whether one particular sentence was true — that is still a reader's job, which is exactly why
the ledger is written down rather than implied: **a false claim beside its label is a claim a later
reader can contradict.** The defect this rule was written from was invisible only because nothing was
written down.
